00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000 align:start position:0%
 
when Apple first announced the 2020  updates to their 13-inch MacBook Pros we  expected the higher-end  $1,800.00 model to have a decent but not  huge improvement in performance over the  base model  thanks to the new 10th gen processor but  with each test that we ran we were  surprised to see more and more  unexpected advantages and reasons to  spend the extra cash on the $1,800 model  and trust me there are more advantages  than you think but before we get into  that as well as the things that  disappointed me with this new MacBook  Pro I want to mention that this does not  make the $1300 base model a bad MacBook  by no means and explain why in my review  of the base model which you can check  out after this video is over if we go  back and take a look at the differences  between the 2019 models they're honestly  wasn't that big of a difference  performance between the two was so close  that we actually recommended either  buying the 13-inch base model or going  all the way up to the 16 inch MacBook  Pro we told people to simply avoid the  $1,800 model but this year things have  completely changed for starters you're  getting a lot more value at that $1,800  price point where it used to come with  the 256 gigabyte SSD and now comes with  512 where it used to come with eight  gigabytes of RAM and now comes with 16  so if you were to buy the base model and  pick up those same upgrades so it can  match what the $1,800 model already  comes with it'll cost you another $300  bringing the price up to $1,600 just 200  bucks shy of the higher-end model so the  question is what do you get for spending  another $200 and picking up the better  model and the answer is a lot but at the  same time there are a couple of really  big disappointments when you're spending  this much cash but let's first start off  with the hidden differences between the  two MacBook Pro models the higher-end  model actually comes with two cooling  fans compared to only one on the base  model which not only helps cool that  more powerful 10th gen down but it also  leads to less fan noise in most tasks we  notice this difference while using both  of these Mac  with an e GPU set up the big difference  here is that the higher-end model has  two extra vents on the bottom for taking  in cool air so while we were using them  in clamshell mode the base models lack  of extra vents cause the single fan to  run faster and louder at a 5800 rpm  during one of our video editing export  tests compared to only 4000 rpm on the  higher-end model now what you might not  expect is that those extra vents on the  bottom actually help with speaker  quality as well since it allows sound to  go down through those vents and bounce  off of your desk increasing volume and  on top of that it physically has better  speakers as well and you can see that  when you take the back casing off so  here's a quick speaker comparison  between the two  [Music]  [Music]  you can definitely hear louder volume  and better speaker quality with the  $1,800 model one more difference is that  you get two more Thunderbolt 3 ports on  the right hand side with the higher-end  model so you can hook up more devices  but even if you don't plan on using all  4 of the ports it's definitely much more  convenient to be able to plug in your  power connector on either side instead  of just the left side like on the base  model now before we get into how the  tension ship surprised us I want to talk  about the new magic keyboard as I'm sure  you've heard hundreds of times Apple  really hit the jackpot with this  keyboard because it feels so much better  than the previous Butterfly model the  key travel is great but it also feels  much more tactile compared to the old  magic keyboard from the 2015 MacBook  Pros I'm also very glad that we now have  a physical Escape key instead of it  being integrated into the touch bar and  the most important thing of all is that  it's finally back to being reliable  thanks to the traditional scissor switch  mechanism now let's finally get into  that I 510 gentian in terms of raw power  using the Geekbench 5 CPU test we didn't  see that big of a difference compared to  the base models i5 chip but in the more  realistic Cinebench r20 stress test we  saw a much larger difference with a 24%  higher score probably due to the better  cooling thanks to the dual fans so the  difference in performance is already  pretty decent compared to the base model  but we're really stands out is graphics  performance and Geekbench 5s medal test  we saw a pretty huge 48 percent higher  score on the $1,800 model which is a big  deal since graphics performance is the  main limitation in the 13-inch MacBook  Pros now what totally surprised us was  that we saw an even bigger difference in  some real-world tests we did some photo  editing and Lightroom classic and when  rendering 50 edited 42 megapixel RAW  files that $1,800 model took three  minutes and 28 seconds compared to four  minutes and 43 seconds for the base over  35% faster and that's only 50 images  now in our 22nd 4k stabilization test in  Final Cut Pro 10 the $1,800 model was  literally twice as fast as the base  model and inner 5-minute 4k eg BC export  test it was again over twice as fast  finishing in just five minutes and ten  seconds now this massive difference  doesn't really make sense since the raw  graphics performance is only 48 percent  faster not over double like what we saw  in these tests yes we know that the base  model only comes with eight gigs of RAM  compared to 16 on the higher-end model  but that's not enough to warrant this  huge of a difference in performance the  best explanation we could find is that  the $1,800 model gets much faster lpddr4  X Ram it clocks in at three thousand  seven hundred and thirty three megahertz  compared to two thousand 133 megahertz  on the base model and because the  graphics chips are integrated into the  CPU itself they borrow memory from the  system's total RAM which is faster on  the higher ed macbook pro so all of  those things could be why we saw a  couple of tests perform so much better  on the $1,800 model now what we also  know is that these chips pack newer  graphics technology like improved  decoders and other graphics tech for  example the $1,800 model can support  apples 6k Pro display XDR because the  tension chips comes with the latest  version of display stream compression  technology and another piece of new tech  is the fact that the Thunderbolt 3  controller is integrated right into the  CPU whereas the hñ processor and the  base model requires an additional  controller chip to make Thunderbolt 3  work this might not seem like a huge  deal until you see our results from our  a GPU comparison video in quite a few  video editing tests our 5700 xde GPU  barely helped the MacBook Pros  performance but we noticed that the  $1,800 model was able to utilize the  same a GPU much better improving speeds  by almost two times in some tests  compared to relatively small  improvements when connected to the base  model and we think is because of that  new inner  Thunderbolt three technology and that  could also be wide the $1,800 model with  the EGP you got an average of 85 FPS in  our fortnight gaming test compared to an  average of 65 fps on the base model so  this shows that the tangential has a lot  of tricks up its sleeve that we weren't  expecting at all and that's why a  recommendation is that if you can afford  the $1,800 MacBook Pro you should 100%  buy it over the base model but for those  who can't  the $1,300 model will still do a decent  job for a lot of different things and  now let's finally get into the downsides  if you're paying $1,800 for a premium  MacBook Pro from Apple you can at least  expect to get decent Hardware across the  board but the 720p webcam is downright  terrible in 2020 this flaw is sticking  out like a sore thumb now that a lot of  people are forced to work online and  meet up with people using their webcam  so when a three-year-old I phones  FaceTime camera looks leaps and bounds  better than the webcam on a brand new  20/20 MacBook Pro there's a problem and  if the $400 surface go to tablet can  come with a much better 1080p webcam why  can't the $1,800 MacBook Pro now I know  there's the issue of the top lid being  too thin but they could at least update  it with a newer 720p sensor that has  better low-light performance for a  brighter sharper image so hopefully  we'll see Apple address this issue in  next year's MacBook Pro and one more  thing I want to complain about is the  lack of a dedicated graphics chip even  though the new integrated graphics chip  has improved performance by quite a bit  it's still by far the limiting factor  when it comes to performance and you  might think that maybe the 13-inch size  is too thin but the new razor blade self  13-inch is actually slightly thinner  than the MacBook Pro and you can buy it  with a new gtx 1650 TI graphics card for  $1,800 total now I'm sure the reason why  Apple hasn't put a dedicated graphics  chip in the 13-inch is because they want  to push people towards the 16 inch model  but they could at least add it in and  make it a more expensive $300 upgrade  and I'm sure people will still pay for  it so those are my two major complaints  with the $1,800 MacBook Pro but if we're  comparing it to the same model last year  you're actually getting quite a lot more  value and surprisingly great benefits  from the new tension processor which  lead to much better performance than  before so as of right now we definitely  recommend spending the extra cash on  this MacBook Pro instead of the base  model if you can afford it but if you  agree with my disappointments you could  be better off waiting until next year to  see if Apple fixes those issues if you  enjoy this review make sure it's Happ  the like button and click the circle  above to subscribe so you don't miss out  on more videos like this one and make  sure to check out my review of the base  model right over there thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  video  [Music]

